<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koumbi Saleh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koumbi Saleh is an archaeological site in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> widely identified as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>. Arabic sources describe a twin city with a Muslim merchant quarter and a royal town. It was a major centre of trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt, and its decline was followed by the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> in the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koumbi-saleh.jpg" alt="Koumbi Saleh" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chloé Capel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_site_of_Koumbi_Saleh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The ruins lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, 30 km north of the Malian border, 57 km south-southeast of Timbédra and 98 km northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. The land is low grass with thorny scrub and the occasional acacia. Rain falls only from July to September and fills a few depressions; for the rest of the year there is no surface water. Koumbi Saleh is also the name of a present-day commune, with a population of 15,084 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2023.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The geographer al-Bakri, writing around 1068 from earlier writers and informants he met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, described the city of Ghana as two towns about six miles apart: a large Muslim town with twelve mosques and a king's town called Al-Ghaba, with a palace and domed dwellings inside an enclosure. In 1913 a French translation of the 17th-century chronicle Tarikh al-fattash, which names Koumbi as the capital of the Kayamagna dynasty, led French archaeologists to these ruins.</p><p>The identification is increasingly disputed. No inscription links the site to the capital, and no trace of the king's town has been found. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Al-Idrisi</a> in the 12th century placed the royal city on a river, which has suggested that the capital moved south. Ann Kritzinger has argued that the site is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> town of Awdaghost, and the archaeologist Kevin MacDonald thought it looked more like an Arab–Berber trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> than an indigenous capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Albert Bonnel de Mézières first reported the ruins in 1914, and Paul Thomassey and Raymond Mauny (1949–1951), Serge Robert (1975–76) and Sophie Berthier (1980–81) dug there after him. They uncovered houses of local schist, some probably of more than one storey, along narrow streets, a wide east–west avenue and a congregational mosque about 46 m by 23 m. Mauny estimated 15,000 to 20,000 inhabitants. Radiocarbon dates run from the late 5th to the 14th century. The site has been on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage Tentative List since 14 June 2001.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koumbi Saleh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The capital, generally identified with <b>Koumbi Saleh</b>, was a double city: the royal town of El-Ghaba with its palace and sacred grove, and a separate Muslim merchants' town six miles distant with twelve mosques and surrounding gardens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Sumanguru Kante, who ruled in the early 13th century, Sosso took <b>Koumbi Saleh</b>, the old capital of Ghana, and subdued the Mande chiefdoms, including the Kingdom of Kangaba.</blockquote><a href="/state/sosso-kingdom">Sosso kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
